──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
1. PIPELINE OVERVIEW
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

The pipeline consists of three main stages:

  Stage A — Query generation
    A VLM (SmolVLM2) watches each video and generates a list of
    detected events in text form. These become the retrieval queries.
    Two prompt strategies were tested and compared.

  Stage B — Moment retrieval
    Each query is fed — along with the video — into a pre-trained
    moment retrieval model. Two models were tested:
      • Moment-DETR (via Lighthouse)
      • CG-DETR    (via Lighthouse)
    Each query is also paraphrased into 3–5 variants; the highest-
    scoring prediction across all variants is kept.

  Stage C — Post-processing & evaluation
    Short/long/out-of-bounds predictions are filtered. Temporal IoU
    is computed against manual annotations for each model.


──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
2. EVALUATION PROCEDURE
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Metric: Temporal IoU (Intersection over Union)
  IoU = |predicted ∩ ground-truth| / |predicted ∪ ground-truth|
  Range: 0.0 (no overlap) → 1.0 (perfect match)

Matching strategy: recall-oriented greedy matching.
  For each GT event, find the predicted window with the highest IoU
  (no 1-to-1 constraint). This reflects how well the model covers
  each annotated moment, regardless of duplicate predictions.

Manual annotations (videos_annotations.txt):
  Format: Description, MM:SS–MM:SS, subjectivity_score
  Subjectivity scores: -2 (irrelevant) to +2 (highly relevant)
  Total: 38 annotated GT events across 4 videos.

Post-processing thresholds applied before evaluation:
  • Minimum segment duration: 2.0 s
  • Minimum confidence score: 0.0 (no filtering on score)
  • Maximum segment duration: 120.0 s
  • Timestamps clamped to [0, video_duration]


