3. CRITICAL DECISION: CHOOSING THE RETRIEVAL FRAMEWORK
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

The assignment required using at least two pre-trained moment
retrieval models. The available options were Moment-DETR,
QD-DETR, CG-DETR, and others, all accessible via the Lighthouse
library. We chose Moment-DETR and CG-DETR for the following reasons:

  Why Lighthouse over raw model repos:
    The individual repos (jayleicn/moment_detr, wjun0830/CGDETR)
    require manual feature pre-extraction: you must run a CLIP or
    SlowFast backbone on every video frame, save the features to
    disk, and pass them to the model. Lighthouse wraps this entire
    pipeline inside a single predictor class that accepts raw video
    and a text query, handles frame sampling, feature extraction,
    and inference internally. This was a deliberate trade-off:
    faster iteration at the cost of less control over the feature
    extraction process.

    Consequence of this decision: we cannot easily swap backbone
    architectures (e.g., from ViT-B/32 to ViT-L/14) without
    switching to a different Lighthouse model checkpoint. If we
    had used the raw repos, we could have tried different CLIP
    scales or combined multiple backbones.

  Why Moment-DETR as model 1:
    Moment-DETR is the foundational model the assignment explicitly
    references. It introduced the DETR-style (detection transformer)
    approach to moment retrieval: the video and query are jointly
    encoded and a set of decoder queries predict (start, end, score)
    tuples directly. It is a reliable baseline and the most widely
    cited model in this space.

  Why CG-DETR as model 2:
    CG-DETR (Contrastive Grouping DETR) extends Moment-DETR by
    conditioning each decoder query on the text query via cross-
    group attention. This makes it more robust to partial or noisy
    queries — exactly the situation we are in, since our queries
    come from a VLM that often hallucinates content.

  Challenge encountered — checkpoint loading on Windows:
    Both models ship their checkpoints in a format that includes
    EasyDict objects (a Python library for attribute-style dict
    access). PyTorch's safe deserialization mode (weights_only=True)
    cannot unpickle EasyDict, raising a runtime error. We resolved
    this by converting the checkpoint once: load with weights_only=
    False, replace opt=EasyDict(...) with opt=dict(...), save the
    plain-dict checkpoint, then patch torch.load to re-wrap opt in
    EasyDict after loading so Lighthouse's internal code still works.
    This was a non-trivial integration fix with no documentation.

  Challenge encountered — CLIP download path on Windows:
    Lighthouse downloads CLIP weights to the user's home directory
    by default (~/.cache/clip). On Windows this can conflict with
    OneDrive or other path issues. We patched clip.load to redirect
    downloads into the project's own weights/ directory, keeping
    the project self-contained and reproducible.


