4. CRITICAL DECISION: VIDEO FEATURE EXTRACTION
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

The assignment states that "Moment-DETR expects pre-extracted
features, not a raw video." This is a fundamental architectural
constraint that shaped our entire pipeline.

  What features are used and why:
    Lighthouse uses CLIP (ViT-B/32) to encode video frames. CLIP
    was chosen because it was trained on 400M image-text pairs and
    produces embeddings that align visual content with natural
    language — exactly what moment retrieval needs. Each video is
    sampled at a fixed frame rate, each frame is passed through
    CLIP's image encoder, and the resulting sequence of 512-d
    vectors is what the transformer sees.

    Alternative we implemented: CLIP+SlowFast (Step 6.12).
    SlowFast R50 adds a dual-pathway temporal backbone that captures
    both slow (semantic) and fast (motion) features at different
    frame rates. The expected advantage is on action-heavy videos
    where distinguishing between similar-looking but temporally
    different motions matters (e.g., run vs. jump vs. climb).

  Impact on the annotation–prediction gap:
    CLIP features encode appearance, not identity. Two consequences:
    1. Named-entity events (David Belle, Charlotte Parkour) cannot
       be distinguished from any other person performing the same
       action. A query "David Belle performs parkour" is functionally
       identical to "a person performs parkour" at the feature level.
    2. Short events (< 5 s) are harder to localise because fewer
       CLIP frames fall within the window, giving the model less
       signal. This is why GT events like "A logo appears introducing
       Charlotte Parkour" (2 s, GT 00:18–00:20) consistently get
       near-zero IoU — the model has no temporal resolution to lock
       onto a 2-second window.

  Critical reflection on the HERO alternative:
    The assignment also suggests HERO feature extraction
    (linjieli222/HERO_Video_Feature_Extractor), which combines
    ResNet visual features with audio features. For interview-heavy
    content (video_21 TV interview, video_22 Charlotte Parkour
    interview), audio cues (speech, silence, music) could
    meaningfully distinguish talking-head segments from action
    segments. CLIP has no audio modality and cannot exploit this.
    Using HERO features would likely improve IoU specifically for
    "social" and "narrative" category events.

  Video chunking decision (max 149 s, 10 s overlap):
    Lighthouse's practical limit is ~150 s per video clip (the
    model's positional encodings degrade for longer sequences and
    GPU memory becomes an issue). Video_21 is 647 s, so we split
    it into 5 overlapping chunks. The 10 s overlap is a deliberate
    decision to prevent events near chunk boundaries from being
    missed — without overlap, an event centred on the 149 s cut
    would appear in neither chunk's "main" region.

    Trade-off: the overlap means each chunk boundary region is
    evaluated twice, producing two competing predictions for any
    event that spans it. Our aggregation step (keep best score
    across all chunks × all query variants) handles this correctly
    but the IoU ceiling for boundary-spanning events is lower than
    for events fully contained in one chunk.

    Reflection: a sliding-window approach with finer granularity
    (e.g., 60 s windows with 30 s overlap) would improve boundary
    coverage but multiply inference time by ~3x. For a 10-minute
    video with 24 events × 5 query variants × 2 models, the current
    approach already requires hundreds of model inference calls.


