6. QUERY DESIGN — COMPARING TWO VLM PROMPT STRATEGIES
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Both prompts instructed SmolVLM2 to list the main events visible
in each video, without providing timestamps. The key difference
was the instruction level of detail.

  Prompt 1 — "List the main events you see in this video."
    Produced concise, action-focused descriptions.
    Example (video_21, event 1):
      "A man is running down a street with buildings behind him."
    Example (video_23, event 1):
      "A person is walking down a staircase with their hands behind them."

  Prompt 2 — More detailed instruction requesting scene context.
    Produced longer, more narrative descriptions — but with
    significantly more hallucination.
    Example (video_22, events 1-3 from prompt 2):
      "A man runs across an open field towards another group of
       men playing frisbee."
      "Two young women walk down a sidewalk near some trees."
      "Three boys play basketball together under a large tree."
    → None of these scenes exist in video_22 (Charlotte Parkour).
      The model completely fabricated outdoor leisure scenes.

Critical reflection on prompt design:
  • Prompt 1 is better: it produces shorter, more grounded queries
    that more often describe something visually present, even if
    vaguely (e.g., "a man running" is at least partially correct
    for a parkour video, even without identifying the athlete).
  • Prompt 2 over-triggers the model's prior knowledge. Given a
    long video and a request for narrative context, SmolVLM2 fills
    gaps with plausible-but-wrong content (frisbee, basketball).
  • Neither prompt can correctly identify named entities such as
    "David Belle" or "Charlotte Parkour" because SmolVLM2 does
    not recognise specific people by face, nor does it reliably
    read text overlaid on screen.
  • The multi-query variant strategy (3–5 paraphrases per event)
    partially compensates for prompt sensitivity. Even if one
    phrasing gives a poor match, another variant may align better
    with what the model was trained to retrieve.


