7.3 Worst Predictions
──────────────────────
  Named-entity events consistently get the lowest IoU:
  • "David Belle performs parkour sequences in an introductory
    montage" (GT 00:08–02:48) — the VLM described "a man running
    down a street", causing the model to retrieve a very different
    region of the video.
  • "A member of Charlotte Parkour is interviewed" (GT 00:21–02:30)
    — a 2-minute interview segment that the VLM compressed into
    zero dedicated events. Neither model predicted a 2-minute window
    because no query pointed to it.

7.4 Why CG-DETR outperforms Moment-DETR
─────────────────────────────────────────
  CG-DETR uses a conditional cross-attention mechanism that attends
  to the query more explicitly at each frame, making it more robust
  when the query text only partially overlaps with the actual video
  content. Moment-DETR uses a simpler bi-directional attention and
  is more sensitive to exact query phrasing. Since our queries are
  VLM-generated (and therefore imperfect), CG-DETR's query-
  conditioned approach is an architectural advantage.

  Exception: video_24. Here Moment-DETR wins (0.2529 vs 0.1937).
  Video_24 is a short compilation of fast cuts — CG-DETR's stronger
  contextual attention may over-smooth across cuts, while
  Moment-DETR's simpler attention picks out individual clips better.

7.5 Confidence scores are not meaningful quality indicators
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Every prediction has a confidence score of 0.96–0.9997.
  This is expected: both models were trained on QVHighlights,
  which consists of well-curated English queries matched to clean
  web videos. Our out-of-distribution queries (VLM hallucinations
  of parkour videos) trigger the model's sigmoid head to saturate.
  A score of 0.9997 does NOT mean the prediction is correct —
  it means the model committed to a region with high certainty
  regardless of actual accuracy. IoU against annotations is the
  only reliable quality measure.


