13. POST-PROCESSING — DECISIONS AND REFLECTION
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Post-processing is applied between the raw model output and
the IoU evaluation. Three filters were applied:

  Filter 1: Minimum duration 2.0 s
    Rationale: predictions shorter than 2 s are almost certainly
    spurious — the model's temporal decoder rarely converges on a
    meaningful 1-second window. In practice, very few predictions
    were removed by this filter (the models tend to predict windows
    of 10–60 s regardless of the actual event duration).
    Reflection: a more aggressive minimum (e.g., 5 s) would have
    removed predictions for the 2-second logo event (video_22
    "A logo appears introducing Charlotte Parkour", GT 00:18–00:20)
    which the model can never recover anyway. This would reduce the
    total event count and inflate the remaining mean IoU slightly.

  Filter 2: Maximum duration 120 s
    Rationale: a predicted window longer than 2 minutes covers more
    than half of most of our videos and is too vague to be useful.
    Reflection: no predictions were actually filtered by this cap —
    the models consistently predicted windows of 20–80 s regardless
    of the query. The cap is a safety net that did not activate.

  Filter 3: Timestamp clamping to [0, video_duration]
    Rationale: for video_21 with 5 overlapping chunks, the absolute
    timestamp reconstruction (chunk_start + relative_offset) can
    occasionally produce a start time slightly before 0 or an end
    time slightly past the video end due to floating-point rounding.
    Clamping ensures valid intervals without discarding the prediction.

  What was NOT implemented but should be:
    Merging overlapping predictions: if two query variants both predict
    windows that overlap by > 0.5 IoU, they could be merged into a
    single more precise window (e.g., their intersection). This is
    mentioned in the assignment as an optional post-processing step.
    In our case, since queries are often pointing to different video
    regions entirely, merging would not help the hallucination problem
    but would reduce duplicate predictions for the correctly-retrieved
    events.


──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
14. BOTTOM LINE
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Overall IoU: Moment-DETR 0.243 / CG-DETR 0.278
  Best single-video IoU: video_22, CG-DETR = 0.4164
  Worst single-video IoU: video_23, Moment-DETR = 0.1463

  The pipeline works end-to-end. IoU of ~0.25–0.28 is reasonable
  for a fully zero-shot pipeline with no domain fine-tuning. However,
  the numbers are not high enough to call the retrieval reliable.

  The dominant bottleneck is the query generation stage, not the
  retrieval models. CG-DETR and Moment-DETR both perform close to
  their ceiling given the quality of input queries — the evidence
  for this is that the best-case IoU (0.95 for well-described action
  events in video_22) is very high, while the average is dragged down
  by hallucinated and named-entity queries.

  If improving one thing: replace SmolVLM2 with a model that has
  explicit temporal grounding and can read on-screen text (e.g.,
  Video-LLaMA, InternVideo, or GPT-4V with timestamps). This would
  reduce hallucination and correctly identify logo/title frames.

  If improving a second thing: switch from confidence-score-based
  aggregation to a calibrated selection strategy, or use ensemble
  voting across query variants to reduce the impact of high-
  confidence wrong predictions.

  CG-DETR is the recommended model for this dataset. Its conditional
  cross-attention is more robust to imprecise queries — the exact
  situation we are in — and outperforms Moment-DETR on 3 of 4 videos.